---
// "Start here" in the CMS (registered in public/admin/cms.js): a short guide for volunteers who edit the site.
// It opens inside the CMS, so it's a plain page: the site's fonts and styles, but no top bar, footer or site
// scripts (they expect the top bar). Search engines are told to skip it.
// The "Where everything is" table is the same as the one in README.md: change both together.
import "@fontsource/sora/400.css";
import "@fontsource/sora/500.css";
import "@fontsource/sora/600.css";
import "@fontsource/sora/700.css";
import "../../styles/global.css";
import "../../styles/prose.css";
import PageHead from "../../components/PageHead.astro";
import { site } from "../../lib/site";

const places = [
  ["Articles", "Every article: title, category, date, author, summary, cover image and text."],
  ["Pages → Home", "The carousel at the top, the tagline, the About band, values, ticker, topics, FAQ and contact section."],
  ["Pages → the other pages", "The heading and intro of each page, and the rest of its text (About, Join, Team, Resources, the legal page, the 404 page)."],
  ["Pages → Interactive", "Every lab and tool on the Interactive page: its card, topic, and the heading and intro on its own page."],
  ["Team members", "Everyone’s profile: photo, roles, bio and links. Articles pick their author from here."],
  ["Resource list", "Every recommended resource, its cost and level, and which ones are featured on the homepage."],
  ["Glossary", "Every glossary term."],
  ["Lab exercises", "The exercises inside the labs: phishing messages, log hunt cases, decoding challenges and more, and the career quiz. The preview lists every exercise with its answer."],
  ["Settings → Site", "Site name, contact email, Discord link, menus, social links and the footer."],
  ["Settings → Categories", "Article and resource categories, glossary topics and team roles."],
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
  <title>Start here | {site.name} CMS</title>
</head>
<body>
  <main class="st">
    <PageHead title="Start here" lead="Everything you need to write and edit CyberHeaven, on one page. It takes about three minutes to read." />

    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose st-body">
        <h2>How saving works</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Saving keeps your work as a draft.</strong> Nothing on the live site changes yet, so save as often as you like.</li>
          <li><strong>When it’s ready, send it for review:</strong> after each save the CMS asks whether to send it for review. Pick <em>Send for Review</em> when it’s ready, or <em>Later</em> to keep it a draft (you can also change it any time with the <em>Status</em> menu next to Save). An editor or maintainer gets a message and looks at it.</li>
          <li><strong>Publishing puts it live.</strong> An editor or maintainer approves a change by setting its status to <em>Ready</em>; then anyone allowed to publish sees <em>Publish</em>, and the site shows the change a few minutes after it’s pressed. If Publish says it couldn’t publish, the change is still waiting for an approval, or a check found a problem.</li>
          <li>The <em>Workflow</em> page in the top bar shows everything that’s being worked on, by status.</li>
          <li>The Save button gets a coloured ring while you have changes that aren’t saved yet. If you try to leave with unsaved changes, the CMS asks first, and keeps a copy in your browser either way.</li>
          <li>No write access yet? You can still edit: the CMS keeps your changes in your own copy of the site (a fork) and sends them for review.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Where everything is</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>In the sidebar</th><th>What you can change there</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{places.map(([where, what]) => <tr><td><strong>{where}</strong></td><td>{what}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Pages and Settings only show up for maintainers, and only maintainers can change someone’s team roles. Want something changed there? Ask on Discord.</p>
        <p>A few things still need code: some labs (Shell quest, Real or fake link, Hash and salt), a new career path in the quiz, and small interface text like search boxes and buttons. Ask on Discord if you want to change one.</p>

        <h2>Writing an article</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The preview on the right shows the article the way the site will, without the animations.</li>
          <li>Above the preview, <em>Before you submit</em> lists what to fix as you type: a missing excerpt or alt text, skipped headings, links to pages that don’t exist, and anything that would stop the article from being published.</li>
          <li>Below the preview you can see how the article will look in Google and when its link is shared. Keep the excerpt under 160 characters so Google shows all of it.</li>
          <li>To add a YouTube video, paste its link on a line of its own.</li>
          <li>A new article starts with an outline. Replace every ✏️ note with your own text: an article can’t be published while one is left.</li>
          <li>To add an interactive widget, or a <em>Tip</em>, <em>Warning</em> or <em>Key takeaways</em> box, use the editor’s <em>Insert</em> menu.</li>
          <li>Tags suggest the ones other articles already use: pick one of those when it fits, so readers find related articles.</li>
          <li>In the preview, dotted words are in the site’s glossary: hover to see the definition.</li>
          <li>Keep lists at the left edge and don’t paste HTML (like tables or embeds copied from another site): the editor can garble it when it saves.</li>
          <li>Images you upload are turned into WebP and shrunk to fit 2000 pixels, so pages stay quick to load.</li>
          <li>To take an article off the site without deleting it, turn on <em>Hide from the site</em>. It shows as <em>Hidden</em> in the list, and turning it off brings it back.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Looking for something to do?</h2>
        <p><em>Content health</em> in the top bar lists small fixes across the site: images without a description, summaries that are too long, profiles without a photo. Pick one, fix it, and send it for review.</p>

        <h2>Words the CMS uses</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Entry:</strong> one thing you edit, like an article or a team member.</li>
          <li><strong>Slug:</strong> the end of a page’s web address (<code>/articles/<em>slug</em></code>). It’s made from the title when you first save. <em>Edit Slug</em> changes it, and old links to the page stop working, so leave it alone once an article is live.</li>
          <li><strong>Creating Article:</strong> the heading of a new article until its first save.</li>
          <li><strong>UTC:</strong> next to dates. The date you pick is the date the site shows; you can ignore it.</li>
          <li><strong>Assets:</strong> the images uploaded to the site. Every article and page picks its images from there.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Text in curly brackets</h2>
        <p>Some text has placeholders like <code>{"{count}"} terms</code>. Keep them exactly as they are: the site fills in the real number when it builds, so it never goes out of date. Each field’s hint says which placeholders it supports.</p>

        <h2>When something goes wrong</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>“Entry Changed by Someone Else”:</strong> someone saved this entry while you had it open. <em>Save Anyway</em> replaces their changes with yours, so don’t pick it unless you’ve checked with them. Copy your changes somewhere, reload, and add them again.</li>
          <li><strong>“Restore Draft”:</strong> the CMS kept changes you hadn’t saved (the tab closed, or you left without saving). Pick <em>Restore</em> to get them back, or <em>Discard</em> to start from the saved version.</li>
          <li><strong>“Couldn’t publish the entry”:</strong> the change still needs an approval, or the checks found a problem. The checks run on GitHub for every change; the <em>Before you submit</em> card catches most of what they look for.</li>
          <li><strong>Deleted something by mistake?</strong> Ask a maintainer on Discord: every version is kept on GitHub, so it can be brought back.</li>
          <li><strong>A team member can’t be deleted</strong> while they have articles. Pick another author for those first.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Need a hand?</h2>
        <p>Ask on <a href={site.discord} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Discord</a> or email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. Nothing you do here is final: every change is kept on GitHub, so a mistake can always be undone.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</body>
</html>

<style is:global>
  /* Shown inside the CMS (an iframe): dark scrollbar, like the CMS around it */
  :root { color-scheme: dark; scrollbar-color: hsl(0 0% 30%) transparent; }
</style>

<style>
  .st { padding-bottom: 96px; }
  .st-body { max-width: 760px; }
  .st-body h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
